=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=28 Dose adjustment for normal eating (DAFNE). n.d. Available: https:// www.dafneonline.co.uk/, ord=205], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0206, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.91, t=65.40509999999995, r=531.9600000000002, b=80.46135000000004}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=29 Ames GE, Heckman MG, Grothe KB, et al. Eating self-­efficacy: development of a short-­form WEL. Eat Behav 2012;13:375-8., ord=206], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0207, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.91, t=82.40009999999995, r=550.0349999999999, b=105.95384999999999}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=30 Spitzer RL, Kroenke K, Williams JBW, et al. A brief measure for assessing generalized anxiety disorder: the GAD-­7. Arch Intern Med 2006;166:1092-7., ord=207], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0208, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.9025, t=107.89260000000002, r=540.6899999999998, b=122.94885}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=31 Kroenke K, Spitzer RL, Williams JB. The PHQ-9: validity of a brief depression severity measure. J Gen Intern Med 2001;16:606-13., ord=208], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0209, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.895, t=124.88760000000002, r=522.0599999999998, b=139.94385}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=32 National Institute for health and care excellence (NICE). n.d. Available: https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance, ord=209], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0210, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.8875, t=141.88260000000002, r=550.4249999999997, b=165.43635000000006}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=33 Cradock KA, ÓLaighin G, Finucane FM, et al. Diet behaviour change techniques in type 2 diabetes: a systematic review and meta-­ analysis. Diabetes Care 2017;40:1800-10., ord=210], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0211, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.88, t=167.37509999999997, r=551.3774999999999, b=190.9288499999999}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=34 Michie S, Abraham C, Whittington C, et al. Effective techniques in healthy eating and physical activity interventions: a meta-­regression. Health Psychol 2009;28:690-701., ord=211], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0212, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.8725, t=192.86759999999992, r=554.1442499999999, b=216.42134999999985}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=35 Bradley C, Todd C, Gorton T, et al. The development of an individualized questionnaire measure of perceived impact of diabetes on quality of life: the Addqol. Qual Life Res 1999;8:79-91., ord=212], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0213, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.865, t=218.3601, r=545.2574999999997, b=241.9138499999999}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=36 Lowther M, Mutrie N, Loughlan C, et al. Development of a Scottish physical activity questionnaire: a tool for use in physical activity interventions. Br J Sports Med 1999;33:244-9., ord=213], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0214, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.8575, t=243.85259999999994, r=536.1224999999998, b=267.4063500000001}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=37 Medical Research Council. DAPA measurement Toolkit. n.d. Available: https://www.measurement-toolkit.org/anthropometry/ subjective-methods/introduction, ord=214], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0215, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.85, t=269.3451, r=552.4649999999997, b=284.40135000000004}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=38 Gormally J, Black S, Daston S, et al. The assessment of binge eating severity among obese persons. Addict Behav 1982;7:47-55., ord=215], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0216, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.8425, t=286.3401, r=548.5274999999997, b=318.39135000000005}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=39 Saunders JB, Aasland OG, Babor TF, et al. Development of the alcohol use disorders identification test (AUDIT): WHO collaborative project on early detection of persons with harmful alcohol consumption. Addiction 1993;88:791-804., ord=216], PassageDto[id=E-0001-0217, section=REFERENCES, kind=list, page=10, bbox={l=308.835, t=320.3301, r=547.2884999999995, b=352.38135000000005}, page_width=595.2760009765625, page_height=793.7009887695312, text=40 U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Common terminology criteria for adverse events (CTCAE) version 5.0. n.d. Available: https://ctep.cancer.gov/protocoldevelopment/electronic_ applications/docs/CTCAE_v5_Quick_Reference_5x7.pdf, ord=217]], claims=[VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0008, quote=Rising levels of overweight and obesity among people with type 1 diabetes (T1D) contribute to insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, retinopathy, cardiometabolic complications and psychological morbidity., scientificClaim=Rising levels of overweight and obesity among people with type 1 diabetes (T1D) contribute to insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, retinopathy, cardiometabolic complications, and psychological morbidity., consumerDraft=For people with type 1 diabetes, being overweight or obese can lead to insulin resistance, high cholesterol, eye problems, heart issues, and mental health concerns., numbers=[], studyDesign=Not applicable (background information from other studies), population=People with type 1 diabetes who are overweight and obese, findingClass=BACKGROUND, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0008, quote=The Manchester Intermittent and Daily diet Type 1 Diabetes App Study (MIDDAS-Type 1) aims to assess the safety, acceptability and feasibility of remotely delivered ILED and CLED programmes for people with T1D and overweight and obesity., scientificClaim=The Manchester Intermittent and Daily diet Type 1 Diabetes App Study (MIDDAS-Type 1) aims to assess the safety, acceptability, and feasibility of remotely delivered intermittent low-energy diet (ILED) and continuous low-energy diet (CLED) programmes for people with type 1 diabetes (T1D) and overweight and obesity., consumerDraft=This study aims to find out if remotely supported low-energy diet programs are safe and practical for people with type 1 diabetes who are overweight or obese., numbers=[], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=People with type 1 diabetes and overweight and obesity, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0009, quote=Twelve participants with T1D and body mass index ≥27.5 kg/m 2 (≥25 kg/m 2 in high-risk ethnic minorities) recruited from an National Health Service (NHS) trust and research register in England will be randomised to a remotely delivered CLED (n=6) or ILED (n=6) for 12 weeks., scientificClaim=Twelve participants with type 1 diabetes (T1D) and body mass index (BMI) ≥27.5 kg/m2 (or ≥25 kg/m2 in high-risk ethnic minorities) recruited from England will be randomised to a remotely delivered continuous low-energy diet (CLED) (n=6) or intermittent low-energy diet (ILED) (n=6) for 12 weeks., consumerDraft=This study will randomly assign 12 participants with type 1 diabetes and overweight or obesity in England to one of two remote diet programs for 12 weeks., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=Twelve, unit=participants, context=total participants], NumberRefDto[value=≥27.5, unit=kg/m 2, context=body mass index], NumberRefDto[value=≥25, unit=kg/m 2, context=body mass index in high-risk ethnic minorities], NumberRefDto[value=n=6, unit=participants, context=CLED group size], NumberRefDto[value=n=6, unit=participants, context=ILED group size], NumberRefDto[value=12, unit=weeks, context=duration of intervention]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=12 participants with type 1 diabetes, male or female, aged 18-70 years, with BMI ≥27.5 kg/m2 and <50 kg/m2 (or ≥25 kg/m2 and <50 kg/m2 in high-risk minority ethnic groups), recruited from an NHS trust and research register in England, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0009, quote=The primary outcome will assess safety (occurrence of severe hypoglycaemia, ketonaemia, ketoacidosis and time above and below target blood glucose)., scientificClaim=The primary outcome of this study will assess safety, specifically the occurrence of severe hypoglycaemia, ketonaemia, ketoacidosis, and time above and below target blood glucose., consumerDraft=The main goal of this study is to check the safety of the diets, looking at severe low blood sugar, high ketone levels, and blood sugar control., numbers=[], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=People with type 1 diabetes and overweight and obesity, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0009, quote=Secondary outcomes include study uptake, retention, dietary adherence, acceptability, intervention delivery fidelity and potential efficacy in relation to change in weight, insulin requirements, glycated haemoglobin, lipids, insulin resistance (estimated glucose disposal rate) and quality of life., scientificClaim=Secondary outcomes of this study include study uptake, retention, dietary adherence, acceptability, intervention delivery fidelity, and potential efficacy in relation to change in weight, insulin requirements, glycated haemoglobin, lipids, insulin resistance (estimated glucose disposal rate), and quality of life., consumerDraft=The study will also look at how many people join and stay, how well they follow the diet, and potential changes in weight, insulin needs, and overall health., numbers=[], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=People with type 1 diabetes and overweight and obesity, findingClass=SECONDARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0015, quote=Limitations include the small sample size, the diets are not isocaloric over the 12-week trial period and the lack of control group., scientificClaim=Limitations of this study include the small sample size, the diets not being isocaloric over the 12-week trial period, and the lack of a control group., consumerDraft=This study has limitations, including a small number of participants, diets that are not calorie-matched, and no comparison group., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=12, unit=week, context=trial period]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=Not stated, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0019, quote=T1D and overweight and obesity contribute to insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, retinopathy, cardiometabolic complications and psychological morbidity., scientificClaim=Type 1 diabetes (T1D) combined with overweight and obesity contributes to insulin resistance, dyslipidaemia, non-alcoholic fatty liver disease, retinopathy, cardiometabolic complications, and psychological morbidity., consumerDraft=Having type 1 diabetes along with being overweight or obese can lead to insulin resistance, high cholesterol, fatty liver, eye problems, heart issues, and mental health concerns., numbers=[], studyDesign=Not applicable (background information from other studies), population=People with type 1 diabetes who are overweight and obese, findingClass=BACKGROUND, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0020, quote=In contrast, there is little research on weight management in T1D. Challenges in T1D include fear of hypoglycaemia and the burden of increased glucose monitoring and insulin adjustment alongside the energy restriction and increased physical activity required for weight loss., scientificClaim=There is little research on weight management in type 1 diabetes (T1D), and challenges in T1D include fear of hypoglycaemia and the burden of increased glucose monitoring and insulin adjustment alongside the energy restriction and increased physical activity required for weight loss., consumerDraft=There's limited research on weight management for type 1 diabetes, with challenges like fear of low blood sugar and the effort of managing glucose and insulin., numbers=[], studyDesign=Not applicable (background information), population=People with type 1 diabetes, findingClass=BACKGROUND, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0026, quote=The safety, acceptability, feasibility and efficacy of CLED (800 kcal (3347 kJ)/day) in T1D is not established. Patients with T1D may be at risk of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), a potentially life-threatening condition as well as severe hypoglycaemia with a CLED. No studies have tested CLED compared with ILED in patients with overweight and obesity and T1D., scientificClaim=The safety, acceptability, feasibility, and efficacy of continuous low-energy diets (CLED) providing 800 kcal (3347 kJ)/day in type 1 diabetes (T1D) is not established. Patients with T1D may be at risk of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), a potentially life-threatening condition, as well as severe hypoglycaemia with a CLED., consumerDraft=The safety and effectiveness of continuous low-energy diets (800 kcal/day) for type 1 diabetes are not yet known, and these diets might pose risks like diabetic ketoacidosis or severe low blood sugar., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=800, unit=kcal, context=CLED energy intake per day], NumberRefDto[value=3347, unit=kJ, context=CLED energy intake per day]], studyDesign=Not applicable (background information/rationale for the study), population=Patients with type 1 diabetes, findingClass=BACKGROUND, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0028, quote=High-frequency remote follow-up has been shown to enhance adherence, and to be equivalent to face-to-face care (matched for frequency), 17 or superior to low-frequency face-to-face care in weight management interventions. 18 Remote follow-up also reduces participant burden to attend face-to-face appointments., scientificClaim=High-frequency remote follow-up has been shown to enhance adherence and to be equivalent to face-to-face care (when matched for frequency), or superior to low-frequency face-to-face care in weight management interventions., consumerDraft=Frequent remote check-ins can help people stick to weight management plans and may be as good as or better than less frequent in-person visits., numbers=[], studyDesign=Not applicable (background information from other studies), population=People in weight management interventions, findingClass=BACKGROUND, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0053, quote=BMI ≥27.5 kg/m 2 and <50 kg/m 2 or ≥25 kg/m 2 and <50 kg/m 2 in high-risk minority ethnic groups, that is, South Asian, black African and African Caribbean., scientificClaim=Participants in this study must have a body mass index (BMI) ≥27.5 kg/m2 and <50 kg/m2, or ≥25 kg/m2 and <50 kg/m2 if they are from high-risk minority ethnic groups, specifically South Asian, black African, and African Caribbean., consumerDraft=To join the study, participants need a BMI between 27.5 and 50 kg/m2, or between 25 and 50 kg/m2 if they are from certain high-risk ethnic backgrounds., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=≥27.5, unit=kg/m 2, context=BMI inclusion criterion], NumberRefDto[value=<50, unit=kg/m 2, context=BMI inclusion criterion], NumberRefDto[value=≥25, unit=kg/m 2, context=BMI inclusion criterion for high-risk minority ethnic groups], NumberRefDto[value=<50, unit=kg/m 2, context=BMI inclusion criterion for high-risk minority ethnic groups]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=Male or female, aged 18-70 years, diagnosed with T1D for 12 months or longer, from England, with specified BMI criteria, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0104, quote=When a participant commences their allocated diet, basal insulin will be reduced by 50% on low-energy days, and 10% on Mediterranean days. Participants' existing insulin to carbohydrate ratios and sensitivity factors will continue., scientificClaim=When a participant commences their allocated diet in this study, basal insulin will be reduced by 50% on low-energy days and by 10% on Mediterranean days., consumerDraft=In this study, when participants start their diet, their basal insulin will be lowered by 50% on low-energy days and by 10% on Mediterranean diet days., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=50, unit=%, context=basal insulin reduction on low-energy days], NumberRefDto[value=10, unit=%, context=basal insulin reduction on Mediterranean days]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=Participants with type 1 diabetes and overweight and obesity in the study, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0129, quote=At their week 12 visit, participants will be invited to attend and consent to a follow-up visit at MFT at 52 weeks. This will capture change in weight, body composition, waist and hip circumference, BP, HbA1c, eGDR, blood lipids and quality of life (WEL-SF, 29 GAD-7, 30 PHQ-9 31 and ADDQoL 35 ) to assess the long-term impact of LEDs on people with T1D and obesity., scientificClaim=An optional follow-up visit at 52 weeks will assess the long-term impact of low-energy diets (LEDs) on changes in weight, body composition, waist and hip circumference, blood pressure (BP), glycated haemoglobin (HbA1c), estimated glucose disposal rate (eGDR), blood lipids, and quality of life in people with type 1 diabetes (T1D) and obesity., consumerDraft=An optional follow-up at 52 weeks will check the long-term effects of low-energy diets on weight, body measurements, blood pressure, blood sugar, and overall well-being for people with type 1 diabetes and obesity., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=12, unit=week, context=initial intervention period], NumberRefDto[value=52, unit=weeks, context=follow-up visit duration]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol (exploratory substudy), population=People with type 1 diabetes and obesity who complete the 12-week intervention, findingClass=EXPLORATORY, limitations=[]], VerifyClaimDto[passageId=E-0001-0133, quote=Recruitment: successful recruitment of 12 patients to the study., scientificClaim=Progression to a future efficacy trial will be considered if this feasibility trial achieves successful recruitment of 12 patients., consumerDraft=If this study successfully recruits 12 patients, it will help decide if a larger study on the diet programs should be done., numbers=[NumberRefDto[value=12, unit=patients, context=recruitment target]], studyDesign=Randomised feasibility trial protocol, population=Patients with type 1 diabetes and overweight and obesity, findingClass=PRIMARY, limitations=[]]]}] as "application/json" with org.springframework.http.converter.json.MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter
